====================================================================
DOMAIN: Contemporary Reassessments and Critical Perspectives
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 7, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Contemporary reassessments of Engels fall into three broad clusters: (1)
ecological rehabilitation, (2) gender and feminist critique, and (3)
biographical and general revaluations around the 2020 bicentenary. The
ecological cluster, associated above all with John Bellamy Foster and the
Monthly Review school, argues that Engels's dialectics of nature and metabolic
rift thinking are indispensable resources for 21st-century Marxist ecology,
directly countering the Western Marxist consensus (Lukacs to Althusser) that
Engels's naturalism was a philosophical embarrassment. Kaan Kangal's
historical-hermeneutic approach (2019, 2020) deconstructs both the "Engels
the contaminator" and "Engels the systematic materialist" camps, arguing for a
more textually grounded middle position. Terrell Carver and Smail Rapic's 2022
volume positions Engels as requiring renewed scholarly engagement specifically
because of the ongoing political relevance of ecology, feminism, and socialist
strategy. The feminist cluster reassesses The Origin of the Family through the
lens of second-wave and social-reproduction feminism: Blackledge (2018)
defends Engels's historicization of women's oppression as salvageable and
productive; Brown (2012, 2021) extends this into a broader reconstruction of
Marxist feminist theory; Carver (2018) argues that conventional intellectual
biography obscures the gendered nature of Marx's and Engels's political
partnerships. Kohei Saito's degrowth communism project (2023) draws selectively
on Engels, repositioning him in debates about ecosocialism and steady-state
economics. The bicentenary literature (2020) showed a general acknowledgment of
the "distortion" thesis but a growing resistance to it: Blackledge (2020,
2025), Arboleda (2025), and Royle (2021) all argue for retrieving Engels from
the Western Marxist caricature.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly interrogates how the philosophy of history attributed to
Engels -- teleological, naturalistic, economically reductive -- stands up to
textual scrutiny and whether the sharp Marx/Engels dichotomy established by
Western Marxism is defensible. The ecological, feminist, and bicentenary
reassessments all touch directly on the question of whether Engels's historical
materialism is a distortion or a legitimate development of the shared project.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The German-language bicentenary scholarship (especially the Marx-Engels-Jahrbuch
and MEGA-oriented reception) is underrepresented in English-language searches.
The reception of Kangal's work by specialists in German idealism (Hegel/Marx
connection) has not been synthesized. Kevin B. Anderson's recent work has not
yielded a specific paper matching the domain search criteria.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
For the synthesis, distinguish clearly between rehabilitations that accept
Engels's naturalism (Foster, Blackledge, Arboleda) and those that work around
it (Saito's degrowth project, which is more Marxo-centric). This line of
tension maps onto the central question of whether Engels's philosophy of history
is a coherent extension of Marx or an independent and distorting intervention.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Ecological rehabilitation: 4 papers -- Foster, Blackledge 2025, Arboleda, Kangal
- Feminist reassessment: 4 papers -- Blackledge 2018, Brown 2012, Brown 2021, Carver 2018
- Bicentenary general revaluation: 3 papers -- Blackledge 2020, Royle, Carver/Rapic 2022
- Critical/ambivalent: 3 papers -- Kangal 2019, Saito 2023, Hunt 2009
====================================================================
}

@article{blackledge2020engelsvs,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Engels vs.\ Marx?: Two Hundred Years of Frederick Engels},
  journal = {Monthly Review},
  year = {2020},
  pages = {21--39},
  doi = {10.14452/mr-072-01-2020-05_3},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Written for the bicentenary of Engels's birth, this article
  surveys the twentieth-century tradition of attributing the deformations of
  official Marxism to Engels's distortion of Marx's thought, tracing that claim
  from Lukacs and Korsch through Western Marxism to the analytical Marx
  scholarship of Carver. Blackledge argues that this attribution is empirically
  and textually mistaken: the alleged fundamental divergences between Marx and
  Engels on dialectics, nature, and historical materialism do not hold up under
  close examination.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a direct defence of Engels against the "distortion" thesis
  that is central to the research project's framing of Marx/Engels divergence in
  philosophy of history. Sets the terms for the 2020 bicentenary debate.

  POSITION: Rehabilitation of Engels as coherent co-author of historical
  materialism, aligned with the Monthly Review school.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-rehabilitation, bicentenary-2020, distortion-thesis, High},
  abstract = {At the bicentenary of his birth, Frederick Engels's reputation as an original thinker is, among Anglophone academics at least, at its nadir. Despite the recent global economic crisis and associated increases in inequality that have tended to confirm Karl Marx and Engels's general critique of capitalism, Marxism is an optimistic doctrine that has not fared well in a context dominated by working-class retreat and demoralization. But if this context has been unpropitious for Marxism generally, criticisms of Engels's thought have a second, quite separate, source. Over the course of the twentieth century, a growing number of commentators have claimed that Engels fundamentally distorted Marx's thought, and that "Marxism" and especially Stalinism emerged out of this one-sided caricature of Marx's ideas.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{blackledge2025engelsdialectic,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Engels, the Dialectics of Nature, and the Birth of ``Marxism''},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {89},
  number = {1},
  pages = {85--98},
  doi = {10.1177/00368237241312552},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Engels's development of a systematic dialectics of
  nature was not an aberration from Marx but a coherent extension of their
  shared materialist project, and that the institutional formation of "Marxism"
  in the Second International owes more to this Engelsian naturalism than the
  Western Marxist tradition acknowledges. Challenges the view that dialectics of
  nature represents a positivist deviation.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of whether Engels's philosophy of
  nature distorts historical materialism, and situates this in the broader story
  of how "Marxism" as a doctrine was constituted, connecting philosophy of
  history to institutional reception history.

  POSITION: Engelsian naturalism as integral rather than deviating.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-rehabilitation, dialectics-of-nature, Marxism-formation, High},
  abstract = {Sven-Eric Liedman’s The Game of Contradictions (2022) sets out to be both the most comprehensive study of Engels’s work on the social implications of 19th-century developments in the natural sciences and the most detailed analysis of this aspect of the Marx–Engels relationship. While noting the richness and depth of Liedman’s research, this essay argues that his critique of Engels as a system builder is one-sided. Liedman’s focus on the negative aspects of system building obscures the positive implications of Engels’s research. Far from damning 20th-century Marxism, Engels’s historical naturalism confirms that his ideas are more relevant than ever. The contemporary environmental crisis is simultaneously a natural and a capitalist crisis, and any adequate attempt to conceptualize it would be well advised to begin from insights both of his and Marx’s works. Unfortunately, despite highlighting overlaps between Marx’s and Engels’s approaches to the relation between social and natural sciences, Liedman’s dismissive approach to Engels’s Dialectics of Nature blinds him to the continuing relevance of this fundamental resource.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2018reproduction,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Frederick {Engels}, Social Reproduction, and the Problem of a Unitary Theory of Women's Oppression},
  journal = {Social Theory and Practice},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {44},
  number = {3},
  pages = {297--321},
  doi = {10.5840/soctheorpract20186439},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends Engels's The Origin of the Family, Private Property and
  the State as a productive resource for social-reproduction feminism, arguing
  that, once its empirical limitations (drawn from Morgan's anthropology) are
  recognised, its analytical core -- the historicisation of women's oppression
  as a form of capitalist social reproduction -- remains both salvageable and
  necessary for a strategy oriented towards women's liberation.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses one of the most contested aspects of Engels's historical
  materialism -- its account of gender and the family -- and argues for Engels
  as a founding text of feminist historical materialism rather than a
  paternalistic diversion from it.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative feminist reading, aligned with social-reproduction
  theory.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-feminist, Origin-of-Family, social-reproduction, High},
  abstract = {In this paper I argue that Frederick Engels’s The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State remains a fundamental resource for anyone wanting to understand the oppression of women as a capitalist form. By re-examining the strengths and weaknesses of Engels’s historicisation of women’s oppression through the lens of the debates opened by second wave feminism, I argue that, once properly understood, we can overcome the limitations of Engels’s book to point to the kind of unitary theory of women’s oppression essential to a strategy adequate to the needs of the struggle for women’s liberation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{kangal2020engels,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Friedrich {Engels} and the Dialectics of Nature},
  year = {2020},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  series = {Marx, Engels, and Marxisms},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-34335-4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A historical-hermeneutic study of Engels's Dialectics of
  Nature that rejects both the "Engels the contaminator" reading of Western
  Marxism and the hagiographic "Engels the systematic genius" reading of
  orthodox Marxism-Leninism. Kangal reconstructs the textual layers of the work
  (authorial, editorial, interpretational) to show that Engels's dialectics is
  less complete and less dogmatic than either tradition claims, while also more
  substantive than dismissive critics allow.

  RELEVANCE: The most detailed recent philosophical analysis of Dialectics of
  Nature, directly engaging the question of whether Engels's philosophy of
  nature is a coherent departure from or development of Marx. Central reference
  for adjudicating the distortion thesis on its strongest ground.

  POSITION: Nuanced revisionist: neither rehabilitation nor dismissal.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-dialectics-of-nature, historical-hermeneutics, revisionist, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{kangal2019intentions,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Engels' Intentions in Dialectics of Nature},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {83},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--243},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2019.83.2.215},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that reading "controversial intentions" into Engels's
  Dialectics of Nature has been a characteristic but uncritical practice in
  Marx scholarship. By examining the textual, editorial, and interpretational
  layers of the work, Kangal demonstrates that Engels's dialectics is both less
  and more than the major traditions claim, and that settling the question
  requires methodological attention to the work's status as an unfinished torso.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a methodological foundation for the broader question of
  how to read Engels's philosophical contributions, directly applicable to
  debates about whether his philosophy of history is continuous with or diverges
  from Marx.

  POSITION: Hermeneutically revisionist: questions the presuppositions of both
  camps in the Engels debate.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-dialectics-of-nature, hermeneutics, distortion-thesis, High},
  abstract = {Reading different or controversial intentions into Marx and Engels’ works has been somewhat a common but rather unquestioned practice in the history of Marxist scholarship. Engels’ Dialectics of Nature, a torso for some and a great book for others, is a case in point. A bold line seems to shape the entire Engels debate and separate two opposite views in this regard: Engels the contaminator of Marx's materialism vs. Engels the self-started genius of dialectical materialism. What Engels, unlike Marx, has not enjoyed so far is a critical reflection upon the relationship between different layers of this text: authorial, textual, editorial and interpretational. Informed by a historical hermeneutic, inquiry into the elements that structure the debate on “Dialectics of Nature,” and into the different political and philosophical functions attached to it, makes it possible to relocate the meaning of “dialectics” in a more precise context. Engels’ dialectics is less complete than we usually think it is, but he achieved more than most scholars would like to admit.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{carver2022engelsc21,
  editor = {Carver, Terrell and Rapic, Smail},
  title = {Friedrich {Engels} for the 21st Century},
  year = {2022},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  series = {Marx, Engels, and Marxisms},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-97138-0},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: An edited volume collecting new essays on Engels's relevance
  for 21st-century debates in ecology, feminism, political strategy, and
  philosophy of science. The volume is framed as a counterweight to the
  received dismissal of Engels in Western Marxist and post-Marxist scholarship,
  arguing that Engels deserves renewed critical engagement on the merits.

  RELEVANCE: Directly represents the bicentenary rehabilitation movement in
  collected form, bringing together contributors from multiple traditions to
  assess what, if anything, is philosophically and politically live in Engels.

  POSITION: Pluralist rehabilitation: critical but generally favourable.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-rehabilitation, bicentenary-2020, edited-volume, High},
  abstract = {Carlos Sánchez has dedicated a lot of thought and ink to two questions: (1) Is there such a thing as “Mexican philosophy”? and (2) If there is such a thing, does it matter? Throughout his career, Sánchez has consistently answered the first question affirmatively.[1]In response to the second, Sánchez has shared that this tradition mattersto himfor personal reasons. Mexican philosophy has enriched his life, providing resources not only for deep philosophical speculation but also for coming to grips with his identity as a Mexican American. Yet, with respect to the question of why this tradition should matterto everyoneregardless of their background, he at one point confessed, “There is, of course, a well-developed and highly nuanced answer to the question as to why one should study Mexican philosophy ‘at all’. But I haven’t found it yet” (2019).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Mexican Philosophy for the 21st Centurydelivers a well-developed, highly nuanced, and compelling answer to the question of why one should study Mexican philosophy “at all.” Sánchez’s central task is to “normalize” Mexican philosophyoutside Mexico, or to demonstrate to the broader world of academic philosophy (especially in the United States) that Mexican philosophy has an original, rich, and overlooked set of tools for addressing human crises.[2]His wager is that the process of normalization will also bring about concrete results. Thanks to normalization, teaching, writing about, and promoting this subfield will not be a “risky practice”: papers on topics in the subfield will more regularly be included in generalist journals and delivered at non-specialized conferences; the field at large will recognize the significant contributions that this subfield has made to our collective pursuit of wisdom; and we will all have access to new “counterhegemonic philosophical interventions”, as well as new resources for expanding our syllabi (2023, 5-6).

According to Sánchez, an obstacle in the way of normalizing Mexican philosophy doubles as one of its characteristic features: it is “post-Western”, simultaneously rejecting and embracing a set of concepts, themes, and methods that have defined the history of Western philosophy. The label “post-Western” is one that Sánchez cleverly coins to capture the unique position of Mexican philosophy as caughtin betweenits homegrown and foreign philosophical foundations, and reducible to neither. The body of Sánchez’s book elaborates upon Mexican philosophy’s post-Western character by way of an itemized exposition of key concepts, themes, and methods. It is broken up into seven chapter-length analyses ofrelajo,nepantla,zozobra,corazonadas,tik, the “figure of the world,” and “Mexistentialism,” respectively.

Chapter 1 analyzes Jorge Portilla’s concept of “relajo”. In the context of Portilla’s phenomenology,relajorefers to a behavior or attitude that disrupts the collective effort to realize value by displacing an audience’s attention. The meaning of life unfolds as we take value seriously, living up to its demands and sincerely engaging in realizing it.Relajotransforms otherwise meaningful pursuits into totally chaotic events. Sánchez artfully illustrates the concept with real-world scenarios, including the January 6, 2021 attack on the United States Capitol, college lectures, town hall meetings, and run-ins with uptight HOA presidents. When a student interrupts a lecture with a joke, and the joke cascades into a commotion (some might ask what the joker said, others laugh, others scoff, etc.), the seriousness of the lecture has been suspended, andrelajohas entered the building.

Sánchez closes this chapter with a fascinating reflection onrelajo’s decolonial potential. Colonization uproots native values, replaces them with values that uphold colonial rule, and codifies the structures that perpetuate those values, so that everyone in that society takes these new values seriously. In this context,relajomight seem like an anti-colonial, decolonial, or outright liberatory maneuver, severing a community’s ties to colonial values. Sánchez cautions that the very structure ofrelajopreventsit fromfullyliving up to its liberatory potential (2023, 48-51). Although, it can perhaps be atemporarymeans of resistance. Accordingly, Sánchez insists that his efforts in this book may contain elements ofrelajo, but his is a more constructive venture.

Chapter 2 turns tonepantla: a Nahua concept that refers to the state of existing in between two identities, temporalities, or ways of being. Sánchez, echoing Emilio Uranga (see 2021, 167), shows us thatnepantlais one of many “autochthonous” concepts that guide an original philosophical program, tracing the term back from 16th-century Indigenous-Spanish dictionaries through the history of Mexican philosophy to the present. Beyond simply providing a descriptive genealogy of the term, Sánchez concludes this chapter by speculating about the prescriptive power ofnepantla.Nepantlaopens us to the possibility that we are not living embodiments of absolute ideals but creatures who permanently exist in between places, times, states, conditions, identities, and so on. As such, Sánchez suggests, it represents a solid foundation for critiquing American individualism, mass consumption, flawed theories of race and sex, and other existential maladies rooted in a misleadingly static picture of existence.

Chapter 3 tackles one of the trickiest concepts to pin down in the history of Mexican philosophy:zozobraor the feeling that accompanies choosing to existin two contradictory states of being simultaneously. Sánchez provides an array of examples that further illustrate this feeling, including a sustained reflection on life during the peak of the COVID-19 quarantine and the experience of simultaneously lovingandhating someone. Sánchez’s treatment of the concept is comprehensive. He lists numerous characteristics Uranga associates with the term and ties each to relevant passages in a handy table (2023, 82-83). He also animates the difference betweenzozobraand other key concepts from European existentialism, such as anxiety, ambiguity, and nausea. Though this chapter is mostly expositional—defining and clarifying the meaning of this important concept—Sánchez emphasizes thatzozobracan serve a social purpose, establishing a foundation for authentic interpersonal connections (2023, 80) and community (2023, 87).

Relajo,nepantla, andzozobraare better-known concepts from the history of Mexican philosophy. Chapter 4 is the first of several sustained analyses of lesser-known concepts. Acorazonadais a heartfelt certainty—not a mere hunch—that results from a direct, intuited, lived experience of one’s circumstances. Etymologically rooted in the Spanish word for “heart” (“corazón”), the term implies “that the heart, and not the mind, is a privileged source of, or access to, justification, truth, or revelation” (2023, 93). One noteworthy feature of this chapter is its careful, historically informed contextualization of the concept. Sánchez contrastscorazonadaswith Kantian, Husserlian, Bergsonian, and Heideggerian senses of “intuition” (§2) and compares it to mundane and esoteric senses of “gut feeling” (§§3-4).},
}

